Q: How to change Flutter app icon dynamically at runtime like Google Calendar? A: <p>Google Calendar's dynamic icon works at the OS level using platform-specific APIs. Flutter doesn't have a built-in way to do this, but you can achieve it using a combination of Android's <code>&lt;activity-alias&gt;</code> and iOS's <code>setAlternateIconName</code>.</p>
<h2>Android: activity-alias approach</h2>
<p>Android doesn't support changing the launcher icon at true runtime. The workaround is to pre-define multiple <code>&lt;activity-alias&gt;</code> entries in your <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code>, each pointing to a different icon, and enable/disable them programmatically.</p>
<p><strong>1. Add your icon variants to <code>android/app/src/main/res/mipmap-*</code> folders</strong> (e.g., <code>ic_launcher_day1.png</code>, <code>ic_launcher_day2.png</code>, etc.)</p>
<p><strong>2. Add activity-alias entries in <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code>:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- Default icon (keep your existing &lt;activity&gt;) --&gt;
&lt;activity
    android:name=&quot;.MainActivity&quot;
    android:icon=&quot;@mipmap/ic_launcher&quot;
    ...&gt;
    &lt;intent-filter&gt;
        &lt;action android:name=&quot;android.intent.action.MAIN&quot;/&gt;
        &lt;category android:name=&quot;android.intent.category.LAUNCHER&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/intent-filter&gt;
&lt;/activity&gt;

&lt;!-- Alternate icon 1 --&gt;
&lt;activity-alias
    android:name=&quot;.MainActivityAlias1&quot;
    android:targetActivity=&quot;.MainActivity&quot;
    android:icon=&quot;@mipmap/ic_launcher_alt1&quot;
    android:enabled=&quot;false&quot;&gt;
    &lt;intent-filter&gt;
        &lt;action android:name=&quot;android.intent.action.MAIN&quot;/&gt;
        &lt;category android:name=&quot;android.intent.category.LAUNCHER&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/intent-filter&gt;
&lt;/activity-alias&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>3. Switch icons from Dart using a MethodChannel:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>// In your Dart code
const platform = MethodChannel('app_icon');

Future&lt;void&gt; changeIcon(String iconName) async {
  await platform.invokeMethod('changeIcon', {'icon': iconName});
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>4. Handle it on the Android side in <code>MainActivity.kt</code>:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>override fun configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine: FlutterEngine) {
    super.configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine)
    MethodChannel(flutterEngine.dartExecutor.binaryMessenger, &quot;app_icon&quot;)
        .setMethodCallHandler { call, result -&gt;
            if (call.method == &quot;changeIcon&quot;) {
                val icon = call.argument&lt;String&gt;(&quot;icon&quot;) ?: &quot;default&quot;
                setIcon(icon)
                result.success(null)
            }
        }
}

private fun setIcon(iconName: String) {
    val pm = packageManager
    // Disable all aliases first
    val aliases = listOf(&quot;.MainActivity&quot;, &quot;.MainActivityAlias1&quot;)
    for (alias in aliases) {
        pm.setComponentEnabledSetting(
            ComponentName(packageName, &quot;${packageName}$alias&quot;),
            PackageManager.COMPONENT_ENABLED_STATE_DISABLED,
            PackageManager.DONT_KILL_APP
        )
    }
    // Enable the selected one
    val target = if (iconName == &quot;default&quot;) &quot;.MainActivity&quot; else &quot;.MainActivityAlias1&quot;
    pm.setComponentEnabledSetting(
        ComponentName(packageName, &quot;${packageName}$target&quot;),
        PackageManager.COMPONENT_ENABLED_STATE_ENABLED,
        PackageManager.DONT_KILL_APP
    )
}
</code></pre>
<h2>iOS: setAlternateIconName</h2>
<p>iOS natively supports alternate icons. Add your alternate icon files to the Xcode project and declare them in <code>Info.plist</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;key&gt;CFBundleIcons&lt;/key&gt;
&lt;dict&gt;
    &lt;key&gt;CFBundleAlternateIcons&lt;/key&gt;
    &lt;dict&gt;
        &lt;key&gt;AppIconAlt1&lt;/key&gt;
        &lt;dict&gt;
            &lt;key&gt;CFBundleIconFiles&lt;/key&gt;
            &lt;array&gt;
                &lt;string&gt;AppIconAlt1&lt;/string&gt;
            &lt;/array&gt;
        &lt;/dict&gt;
    &lt;/dict&gt;
&lt;/dict&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then call from the iOS MethodChannel handler:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>UIApplication.shared.setAlternateIconName(&quot;AppIconAlt1&quot;)
</code></pre>
<h2>Package option</h2>
<p>If you don't want to write platform code yourself, the <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_dynamic_icon" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>flutter_dynamic_icon</code></a> package wraps both Android and iOS implementations and handles the MethodChannel setup for you.</p>
<p><strong>Important limitation:</strong> On Android, changing the icon causes a brief delay (1-2 seconds) where the launcher refreshes. This is an OS-level behavior and cannot be avoided. Google Calendar gets around this because it uses a system-level widget, not the activity-alias trick.</p>

Q: NextJS Tailwind Global.css parsing failure - removed issue but still unable to parse A: <p>I ran into the same <strong>global.css parsing error</strong>. Tried reverting to a working backup and still had the same issues. Then I realised related to <strong>Turbopack</strong>, not <strong>Tailwind</strong> itself. I disabled <strong>Turbopack</strong> and switched back to the standard <strong>Next.js dev server</strong>.</p>
<h3><strong>Update <a href="/questions/tagged/package.json" class="s-tag post-tag" title="show questions tagged &#39;package.json&#39;" aria-label="show questions tagged &#39;package.json&#39;" rel="tag" aria-labelledby="tag-package.json-tooltip-container" data-tag-menu-origin="Unknown">package.json</a></strong></h3>
<p>Change:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;dev&quot;: &quot;next dev --turbopack -p 3000&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>To:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;dev&quot;: &quot;next dev -p 3000&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Make sure to use your correct port.</p>
<h3><strong>Next Delete:</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>.next</strong></p>
</li>
<li><p>and <strong>node_modules</strong> folders</p>
</li>
<li><p>and <strong>package-lock.json</strong> file</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Then run a new clean reinstallation:</h3>
<pre><code>npm install
</code></pre>
<p>Just incase, I also added this to to my next.config file to stop any Turbopack related config from interfering:</p>
<pre><code>import type { NextConfig } from 'next';

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  turbopack: {
    resolveAlias: {},
  },
  webpack: (config) =&gt; {
    return config;
  },
};

export default nextConfig;
</code></pre>

Q: Convert a Canvas element into Image element A: <pre><code>const canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;);
const ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);

const img = new Image();
img.src = &quot;image.jpg&quot;;

img.onload = () =&gt; {
  ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  ctx.font = &quot;30px Arial&quot;;
  ctx.fillStyle = &quot;white&quot;;
  ctx.fillText(&quot;TOP TEXT&quot;, 50, 50);
  ctx.fillText(&quot;BOTTOM TEXT&quot;, 50, canvas.height - 20);
</code></pre>

Q: Keycloak SSO Session Idle always set to 30 minutes A: <p>I faced the same issue from Client Side. After Refreshing Tokens in Client Side I was able to solve the issue. I used the following code. This could help. Kindly check.</p>
<pre><code>keycloak.onTokenExpired = () =&gt; {
   console.log(&quot;&gt;&gt;&gt; Re-Authenticated&quot;);
   keycloak.updateToken(50);
};
</code></pre>
